    "text": "# **C Additional Experimental Details** \n\n## **C.1 Uncertainty Propagation using Lipschitz Continuity** \n\nIn this section, we detail our implementation of the Lipschitz-based uncertainty propagation method which we compare with in Section 6. For these experiments, consider the dynamical system \n\n\n\nNote that we drop the dependence on the control input **_u_** _k_ for conciseness, and since our comparisons concern a sequence of known open-loop controls. For simplicity, we assume **_h_** is an affine map, and **_g_** is Lipschitz continuous, such that for all **_x_** _,_ **_µ_** _∈_ R<sup>_n_</sup> , \n\n\n\nThe method presented in [13] consists of propagating ellipsoidal sets: \n\n**Definition 2** (Ellipsoidal Set) **.** A set _B_ ( **_µ_** _,_ **_Q_** ), **_µ_** _∈_ R<sup>_n_</sup> _,_ **_Q_** _∈_ R<sup>_n×n_</sup> _,_ **_Q_** _≻_ 0, is an ellipsoidal set if \n\n\n\nAssume that **_x_** _k ∈B_ ( **_µ_** _k,_ **_Q_** _k_ ). The problem consists of computing **_µ_** _k_ +1 _,_ **_Q_** _k_ +1 such that **_x_** _k_ +1 _∈ B_ ( **_µ_** _k_ +1 _,_ **_Q_** _k_ +1). Generally, the reachable set of **_f_** ( **_x_** _k_ ) given that **_x_** _k_ lies in an ellipsoidal set will not be an ellipsoidal set. However, an outer-approximation is sufficient for control applications where constraints satisfaction needs to be guaranteed. First, we compute the center of the ellipsoid as \n\n\n\nSince **_h_** is affine, its Jacobian does not depend on **_x_** . Thus, we decompose the error to the mean as \n\n\n\nFirst, given **_x_** _k ∈B_ ( **_µ_** _k,_ **_Q_** _k_ ), we have _∇_ **_h_** _·_ ( **_x_** _k −_ **_µ_** _k_ ) _∈B_ ( **0** _,_ **_Q_** nom _,k_ ), with **_Q_** nom _,k_ = **_hQ_** _k_ **_h_**<sup>_T_</sup> . Second, we use the Lipschitz property of **_g_** to bound the approximation error component-wise as \n\n\n\nwhere _λ_ max( **_Q_** _k_ ) denotes the largest eigenvalue of **_Q_** _k_ , and since **_x_** _k ∈B_ ( **_µ_** _k,_ **_Q_** _k_ ). This defines a rectangular set in which **_g_** ( **_x_** _k_ ) _−_ **_g_** ( **_µ_** _k_ ) is guaranteed to lie, which can be outer-approximated by an ellipsoid as \n\n**_g_** ( **_x_** _k_ ) _−_ **_g_** ( **_µ_** _k_ ) _∈B_ ( **0** _,_ **_Qg_** _k_ ) _,_ where **_Qg_** _k_ = _n ·_ diag�( _Lgiλ_ max( **_Q_** _k_ )<sup>2</sup> ) _, i_ = 1 _,_ ... _, n_ � _,_ (14) where diag(...) denotes the diagonal matrix with diagonal components (...). Finally, the two terms can be combined as \n\n\n\nwhere **_Q_** _k_ +1 =<sup>_<u>c</u>_</sup><sup><u>+1</u></sup> _c_<sup>**_Q_**nom</sup><sup>_,k_+ (1 +</sup><sup>_c_)</sup><sup>**_Qg_**</sup><sup>_k_, with</sup><sup>_c_=</sup> �Tr( **_Q_** nom _,k/_ Tr( **_Qg_** _k_ ), and Tr( _·_ ) denotes the trace operator. Finally, combining the terms above and by linearity, \n\n\n\nStarting from **_x_** 0 _∈B_ ( **_µ_** 0 _,_ **_Q_** 0), and applying this recursion for all _k_ = 0 _,_ ... _, N −_ 1, this method enables the computation of sequence of sets which outer approximate the true reachable sets of the nonlinear system, given known upper-bounds for the Lipschitz constant of the dynamics. \n\n14 \n\n"
